# Untitled

## Ternary Polymer Solar Cells Facilitating Improved Efficiency and Stability
The use of a ternary active layer offers a promising approach to enhance the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of polymer solar cells (PSCs) via simply incorporating a third component. Here, a ternary PSC with improved efficiency and stability facilitated by a new small molecule IBC-F is demonstrated. Even though the PBDB-T:IBC-F-based device gives an extremely low PCE of only \(0.21\%\), a remarkable PCE of \(15.06\%\) can be realized in the ternary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F with \(20\%\) IBC-F, which is \(\approx10\%\) greater than that \((\mathsf{P C E}=13.70\%)\) of the control binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S. The improvement in the device performance of the ternary PSC is mainly attributed to the enhancement of fill factor, which is due to the improved charge dissociation and extraction, suppressed bimolecular and trap-assisted recombination, longer charge-carrier lifetime, and enhanced intermolecular interactions for preferential face-on orientation. Additionally, the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F shows better thermal and photoinduced stability over the control binary device. This work provides a new angle to develop the third components for building ternary PSCs with enhanced photovoltaic performance and stability for practical applications.
Solution-processable bulk-heterojunction (BHJ) polymer solar cells (PSCs) are considered a promising solar energy technology, owing to their unique features of easy fabrication, light weight, flexibility, and semitransparency.[1–6] In recent years, the emergence of narrow bandgap n-type organic semiconductor (n-OS)
The \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) can be easily tuned by varying the content of the third component in the ternary PSCs with parallel and alloy working mechanisms.[26–28] As opposed to achieving complementary absorption and matching energy levels, it is a greater challenge to control the morphology in the ternary blend to improve the FF in the ternary device owing to the fact that the third component may act as a recombination center or a morphological trap within the host binary systems. Therefore, it is critical to select a miscible and compatible third component that can function as a morphological modulator to facilitate better phase separation and molecular packing to achieve a higher \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF in ternary PSCs.[29–31] Very recently, PCEs over \(16\%\) were achieved in the ternary PSCs using Y6 as the acceptor.[32]
In addition to a high efficiency, achieving high device stability is critical but is still a significant challenge for the practical application of PSCs. Generally, the device stability suffers from the oxidation of the electrodes, degradation of the interface layers, and intrinsic instability of the active layer morphology driven by light and thermodynamics.[33–39] Apart from elevating the photovoltaic performance, the use of ternary PSCs has a positive effect in strengthening the morphology stability of the BHJ layer for improving the device stability, which mainly arises from the interactions of multiple compositions for the formation of a locked morphology.[40–42] For instance, the ternary PSC with 20 wt% PBDB-T demonstrated
better device stability than the control FTAZ:IT-M-based binary device after a storage time of \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) in a nitrogen-filled glovebox, which was ascribed to the suppressed crystallization of IT-M by the addition of PBDB-T in the blend.[38] In the case of the P3HT:IDTBR-based binary device, the crystalline IDTBR phase was effectively suppressed by the incorporation of IDFBR for preserving a three-phase microstructure, and thus enhanced device stability was realized in the ternary device, so that the P3HT:IDTBR:IDFBR-based ternary device retained \(80\%\) of its initial PCE, whereas the ternary device based on P3HT:IDTBR showed poorer stability with retaining \(70\%\) after \(1200~\mathrm{h}\) in air and under dark conditions.[39] Kim et al. found that the introduction of a small amount of PCBM into the PBDTTTPD:P(NDI2HD-T) all-polymer host blend favored the formation of an intermixed phase, which was beneficial for maintaining stability under thermal stress for PSCs.[40]
To realize the high-performance of the ternary device, the most common strategy is the rational incorporation of a compatible third component into the host blend. It is widely recognized that the third component should have good photovoltaic properties in the corresponding binary device for improving the efficiency of the ternary PSCs. Besides, the addition of some third component with low efficiency in binary blend also showed positive effect on increasing the efficiency, as shown in Table S1 in the Supporting Information.[16–20] For
instance, Ge et al. reported a PBDB-T:ITM:ITCN-based ternary PSC with a PCE of \(12.16\%\) , which is higher than the PBDB-T:ITM \((\mathrm{PCE}=10.89\%)\) and PBDB-T:ITCN-based binary device \((\mathrm{PCE}=2.21\%)\) . Zhan et al. demonstrated a high performance ternary PSC with incorporating \(25\%\) third component TR into the binary PTB7-Th:FOIC host blend, the PCE was improved from \(12.10\%\) for the binary device to \(13.10\%\) for the ternary device, despite the low PCE of \(0.66\%\) for the TR:FOIC-based device.[43] In this contribution, we report a ternary PSC with improved efficiency and stability facilitated by a third component IBC-F (as shown in Figure 1a) with extremely poor photovoltaic performance in the binary devices. IBC-F has the typical \(\mathrm{A-}\pi\mathrm{-D-}\pi\mathrm{-A}\) ( \(\mathrm{~A~}=\) acceptor unit and \(\mathrm{~D~}=\) donor unit) main-chain architecture with a central indacenodithiophene (IDT) donor unit, alkyl cyanoacetate end-capped groups, and fluorine substituted phenyl units as the \(\pi\) -bridge. The fluorine substituents in a \(\pi\) -bridge can potentially promote the macromolecular planarity via weak \(\mathrm{F}\cdot\cdot\mathrm{S}\) intramolecular noncovalent Coulombic interactions and enhance the inter/intramolecular interactions.[44–46] Density functional theory (DFT) calculation results reveal that IBC-F with fluorine substituted phenyl units has relatively high planarity of the backbone (Figure 1a). PBDB-T:IE4F-S (Figure 1a)[47] was chosen as the host blend, where the same central IDT unit in the \(n\) -OS acceptor IE4F-S and IBC-F could potentially ensure their miscibility. The IBC-F can act as
a sensitizer to deliver energies to the PBDB-T:IE4F-S host blend via the Dexter or Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET).[48] In comparison with the control PSC based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) with a PCE of \(13.70\%\) , the ternary PSC with incorporating \(20\%\) IBC-F into the PBDB-T:IE4F-S host blend (PBDBT:IE4F-S:IBC-F \(=1{:}1{:}0.2\) , w/w/w) demonstrated a significantly increased PCE of \(15.06\%\) , despite the extremely low PCE of \(0.21\%\) obtained for the PBDB-T:IBC-F-based binary device. The enhancement of photovoltaic performance of the ternary PSC mainly results from the significantly enhanced FF, which is due to the improved charge dissociation and extraction, suppressed bimolecular, and trap-assisted recombination, longer charge carriers lifetime, and favorable fibrillar morphology along with the enhanced intermolecular interactions for preferential face-on orientation in the ternary blend. Furthermore, the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F exhibits better thermal and photoinduced stability over the binary device, resulting in the ternary device retaining \(81.0\%\) and \(85.5\%\) of its initial PCE after continuous thermal aging and illumination in an inert atmosphere for \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) , respectively, while the PBDB-T:IE4F-S-based binary devices retain a lower initial PCE of \(71.7\%\) and \(73.6\%\) , respectively. These results suggest that the addition of IBC-F into the PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend not only improves the photovoltaic performance but also has a synergistic benefit for enhancing both the thermal aging and photoinduced stability.
The synthetic routes toward IBC-F are shown in Figure 1b and the detailed synthesis procedures are provided in the Supporting Information. The \(n\) -OS acceptor IE4F-S was synthesized according to our previous work,[47] IBC-F exhibits good solubility in common organic solvents, such as chloroform, toluene, and chlorobenzene. As shown in Figure 2a, the UV–vis absorption spectrum of IBC-F in the thin film state mainly appears in the wavelength range of \(450{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}\) , with a maximum peak at \(542\ \mathrm{nm}\) and an optical bandgap of \(2.07~\mathrm{eV}\) . IBC-F exhibits a good absorption profile that is complementary to the PBDBT:IE4F-S blend (Figure 2a). Figure 2b shows the absorption spectra of the PBDB-T:IE4F-S host blend with various contents of IBC-F. Although incorporating \(20\%\) IBC-F into the PBDBT:IE4F-S host blend slightly decreases the absorption coefficient in the wavelength region of \(570{-}760\ \mathrm{nm}\) , the enhanced absorption coefficient can be observed in the short wavelength range of \(440{-}570\ \mathrm{nm}\) , leading to the similar absorption capability of the two blends. This trend becomes more pronounced as the increase of IBC-F contents. The absorption capability of the ternary blends significantly decreased in the wavelength region of \(550{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}\) , while enhanced in the short wavelength of \(450{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) , accompanying with gradually blueshifted low-energy absorption band, which may be due to the deteriorated packing affected by the increased content of IBC-F. The incorporation of \(10\%\) IBC-F in the host blend leads to a lower absorption coefficient in the region of \(550{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}\) , whereas barely influences the short wavelength absorption, which is different from the ternary blends with \(20\%\) or more IBC-F contents. The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels of IBC-F measured by cyclic voltammetry to be \(-5.62\) and \(-3.53\,\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively (as shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information). As depicted in Figure 2c, IBC-F shows deeper HOMO and LUMO levels than PBDB-T but higher LUMO and lower HOMO than
the \(n\) -OS acceptor IE4F-S, indicating incompatible energy levels between IBC-F and IE4F-S in terms of efficient charge separation. We then performed photoluminescence (PL) measurement to examine the working mechanism in the PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F ternary blend. As shown in Figure 2d, the PL emission spectrum of IBC-F mostly overlaps with the absorption spectrum of PBDB-T, which fulfills the requirement of sufficient overlap between the emission spectrum of the energy donor and absorption spectrum of the energy acceptor for FRET theory, indicating that FRET could occur in the PBDB-T:IBC-F blend.[44] To further confirm the existence of FRET in the blend film, the PL spectra of the thin film of PBDB-T, IBC-F, and PBDB-T:IBC-F (1:0.2, w/w) were measured (as shown in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information). When excited at \(580\;\mathrm{nm}\) , the emission of PBDB-T in the PBDB-T:IBC-F (1:0.2, w/w) blend is enhanced compared to the pristine PBDB-T film, suggesting energy transfer from IBC-F to PBDB-T rather than charge transfer. The FRET is further confirmed using time-resolved transient absorption spectroscopy. As shown in Figure 2e, the pristine IBC-F and PBDB-T films show clear ground state bleaching (GSB) signals at \(550\;\mathrm{nm}\) (completely decays \({\approx}1.40\,\mathrm{ps}\) ) and \(640\;\mathrm{nm}\) (completely decays \({\approx}1.10\) ps), respectively. In comparison with pristine IBC-F and PBDB-T, introducing \(20\%\) of the acceptor PBDB-T into the donor IBC-F slightly reduces the decay time for the GSB of IBC-F and prolongs it for the GSB of PBDB-T in the blend (as shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information). This trend becomes more obvious with the increase in acceptor PBDB-T content in the blend, where the GSB signal of IBC-F in the IBC-F:PBDB-T (1:0.8, w/w) blend decays at \({\approx}0.27\) ps, along with a longer decay time at \({\approx}4.24\) ps for the GSB of PBDB-T (Figure 2e), confirming the existence of an energy transfer process from IBC-F to PBDB-T. Figure 2c presents an energy diagram outlining the possible pathways to current generation in the PBDB-T:IBC-F:IE4F-S ternary blend.
PSCs were fabricated with the conventional device structure of glass/indium-tin oxide (ITO)/poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(4-styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS)/active layer/PDINO/Al to investigate the photovoltaic. The optimal treatment condition for the PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) based control device is thermal annealing at \(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{\min.}^{[47]}\) We then fabricated ternary PSCs by incorporating various contents of IBC-F into the PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) host blend. The key photovoltaic parameters of the optimal ternary and binary devices are summarized in Table 1 and the corresponding current density–voltage \((J\!\!-\!\!V)\) curves are shown in Figure 3a.
The binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S exhibits a PCE of \(13.70\%\) and an FF of 0.69, which is in line with our previous work.[47] While the binary PSCs based on PBDB-T:IBC-F (with IBC-F as acceptor) and IBC-F:IE4F-S (with IBC-F as donor) show very low PCE of \(0.21\%\) and \(0.045\%\) , respectively (as shown in Table S2 and Figure S4 in the Supporting Information), indicating the poor photovoltaic performance of IBC-F used as either a donor or acceptor in the binary devices. Notably, with the incorporation of \(20\%\) IBC-F into the host blend, the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) increased from 0.869 to 0.887 V and more importantly a higher FF of 0.744 was achieved, leading to a higher PCE of \(15.06\%\) , which is \({\approx}10\%\) greater than the corresponding binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S. PCEs greater than \(14\%\) were also obtained for the ternary devices with the IBC-F contents of
\(10\%\) and \(30\%\), mainly resulted from the improved FF of 0.734 and 0.724, respectively. With further increasing the IBC-F content over \(30\%\) \((30{-}100\%)\) into the ternary systems, the FF can still reach higher than 0.70, indicating the significantly positive effect of IBC-F third component in improving the FFs of the ternary devices (Figure 3b). The ternary device with \(100\%\) IBC-F (weight ratio of PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F \(=1:1:1\)) demonstrates a PCE of \(11.58\%\), with an FF of 0.70 and a high \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.898\ \mathrm{V}\). The \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of a PSC also depends on the balance of charge generation and recombination except the energy gap between the
HOMO level of donor and LUMO energy level of the acceptor. Although the energy gap \((\Delta E=1.80\ \mathrm{eV})\) between the HOMO of PBDB-T and LUMO of IBC-F is large enough to obtain a high \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) in a PSC, an unexpected low \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.887\ \mathrm{V}\) with large voltage loss of \(0.71\ \mathrm{V}\) was observed in the PBDB-T:IBC-F-based device. We speculate that this is mainly due to the excessive charge recombination in the PBDB-T:IBC-F blend. Note that the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) values of ternary devices concomitantly increase with increasing IBC-F content in the host blend and the ternary device with \(100\%\) IBC-F exhibit the highest \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.898\ \mathrm{V}\),
which is \(0.029\,\mathrm{~V~}\) greater than that of the binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S. The improved \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) in the ternary devices should be ascribed to the higher LUMO level of IBC-F compared to IE4F-S and the good miscibility of IBC-F and IE4F-S, which is confirmed by the simulation (as shown in Figure S5 in the Supporting Information).[49] In comparison with the PBDBT:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) based binary device \((J_{\mathrm{sc}}=22.85~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2})\) , a comparable high \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(22.83\;\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) was yielded in the PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) based ternary device, which should result from the similar light-harvesting capability of the active layer (Figure 2b). Due to the significantly decreased absorption capability of other ternary blends in the wavelength of \(550{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b), the corresponding ternary devices exhibited lower \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) values than the control binary device. Although IBC-F showed appropriate energy levels with
PBDB-T for charge separation in this case, IBC-F acts as a sensitizer that delivers energies to PBDB-T rather than charge transfer. Thus, an extremely low PCE of \(0.21\%\) is obtained in the PBDB-T:IBC-F-based device. In spite of the poor performance of the third component, a notable improvement in efficiency was realized in the ternary device upon introduction of \(20\%\) IBC-F in the host blend, which is rarely observed in highly efficient ternary PSCs.
The external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves of the PSCs are shown in Figure 3d. The \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) values obtained from \(J{-}V\) measurements are rather consistent with the integral \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) values calculated from the EQE curves
with a margin of less than \(7\%\) . The ternary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) showed a nearly identical EQE spectrum with the binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w). Similar to the trend observed in the UV–vis absorption spectra in Figure 2b, loading higher contents of \(30–100\%\) of IBC-F into the ternary system gradually decreased the EQE values of the devices, which is consistent with the decreased \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) values from \(J{-}V\) measurements. High EQE values greater than \(80\%\) in the wavelength region of \(550{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}\) were obtained for both devices based on the binary PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend and the ternary PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) blend, while the EQE values of the ternary PSCs with less or more than \(20\%\) IBC-F were lower than \(80\%\) . This result can be explained by the lower absorption coefficient of the ternary PSCs with IBC-F content other than \(20\%\) in the wavelength region of
550–800 nm (Figure 2b) in comparison with that of the binary PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) and ternary PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) blends.
Generally, the competition between charge transport/extraction and recombination determines the FF of a PSC device. To understand the effect of incorporating IBC-F on improving the FF of the ternary PSCs, the charge transport and extraction properties of the blend films were investigated (as shown in Figure S6 in the Supporting Information). Interestingly, both high hole \((\mu_{\mathrm{h}})\) and electron \((\mu_{\mathrm{e}})\) mobilities of \(1.34 \times 10^{-4}\) and \(2.43 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) were determined for the IBC-F pristine film, respectively, which were measured by a space charge limited current method (Figure S6 in the Supporting Information). The host PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend exhibits high \(\mu_{\mathrm{h}}\) and \(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}\) of \(5.56 \times 10^{-4}\) and of \(5.98 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively, with a \(\mu_{\mathrm{h}}: \mu_{\mathrm{e}}\) ratio of 0.93 (Table S3 in the Supporting Information). The introduction of \(10\%\) of IBC-F into the PBDB-T:IE4F-S host blend decreased both the hole and electron mobilities of the blend to \(3.09 \times 10^{-4}\) and \(3.99 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively. Interestingly, a higher \(\mu_{\mathrm{h}}\) of \(5.34 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and \(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}\) of \(5.09 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) was obtained when the IBC-F content increased to \(20\%\), and then the hole and electron mobilities gradually decreased with further increase the IBC-F contents from \(30\%\) to \(100\%\) (as shown in Figure 4a, Figure S6, and Table S3 in the Supporting Information). Although the introduction of IBC-F in the PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend has a negative effect on improving the transport properties, the PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) ternary blend shows a more balanced \(\mu_{\mathrm{h}}: \mu_{\mathrm{e}}\) ratio of 1.05 than the host blend (Figure 4a and Table S3
in the Supporting Information) so that the corresponding ternary PSC showed higher FF and higher PCE values.
The PL measurements were carried out to investigate the energy transfer and exciton dissociation of the host binary blend and ternary blends (as shown in Figure S7 in the Supporting Information). Relative to the PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) host binary blend with a quenching efficiency of \(80.6\%\) (excited at \(580 \, \mathrm{nm}\)), a higher quenching efficiency of \(90.9\%\) was observed in the ternary blend with \(20\%\) IBC-F, suggesting more efficient exciton dissociation in the PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) ternary blend. While further increment of IBC-F contents led to lower quenching efficiency than the host blend (Figure S7 in the Supporting Information). In comparison with the ternary devices with IBC-F contents other than \(20\%\), a better charge transport properties combining with a higher absorption coefficient and higher PL quenching efficiency lead to the a higher \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of the ternary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w). We then plotted the curves of \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) (\(J_{\mathrm{ph}} = J_{\mathrm{L}} - J_{\mathrm{D}}\), where \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{D}}\) represent the light and dark current densities, respectively) versus the effective voltage (\(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\)) of the devices based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) and PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) to further understand the influence of IBC-F on their exciton dissociation probability (\(P_{\mathrm{diss}}\)) (Figure 4b). It was assumed that all generated electron–hole pairs were dissociated into free charges and collected at the electrodes when \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) reached saturation (\(V_{\mathrm{sat}}\)) at a large reverse bias (\(V_{\mathrm{eff}} = 2 \, \mathrm{V}\) in this case), and \(P_{\mathrm{diss}}\) is determined by normalizing \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) with respect to \(J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) (\(U_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}\)).[50] In comparison with the control binary device with \(P_{\mathrm{diss}}\) of \(95.8\%\), a higher \(P_{\mathrm{diss}}\) value of
\(98.7\%\) was obtained in the ternary PSC with \(20\%\) IBC-F. The results suggest that the incorporation of \(20\%\) IBC-F in the host blend facilitates efficient charge dissociation and extraction, contributing to the enhanced FF value in the resulting device.
In addition to efficient charge dissociation and extraction, a high FF in the PSCs should also originate from the fine-tuned recombination dynamics in BHJ composites. Thus, we measured the dependence of \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{sc}}\) as a function of the light intensity \((P_{\mathrm{light}})\) to obtain deeper insight into the charge recombination kinetics in the binary and ternary devices. In PSCs, the relationship between the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and \(P_{\mathrm{light}}\) \((J_{\mathrm{sc}}\,\propto\,P^{\alpha}{}_{\mathrm{light}})\) can be applied to evaluate the degree of bimolecular recombination in PSCs, where \(\alpha\) is an exponential factor that should be equal to 1 if all charge carriers are swept out before recombination.[51] As shown in Figure 4c, the \(\alpha\) values were fitted to 0.954 and 0.958 for the devices based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) and PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w), respectively. The results indicate very weak bimolecular recombination in the two devices and the slight impact of \(20\%\) IBC-F on restricting bimolecular recombination in the ternary system. The slope of the dependence of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) versus logarithmic of \(P_{\mathrm{light}}\) \((V_{\mathrm{oc}}\,\propto\,\ln P_{\mathrm{light}})\) can be fitted to determine the degree of trap-assisted recombination in the two devices, a slope of \(1\times k T/q\) ( \(k\) is Boltzmann's constant, \(T\) is temperature, and \(q\) is the elementary charge) implies the freecarrier bimolecular recombination in the device, while a slope of \(2\times k T/q\) suggests that trap-assisted or Shockley–Read–Hall recombination is the dominating mechanism.[52] Compared to the slope of \(1.171\times k T/q\) for the device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w), a smaller slope of \(1.140\times k T/q\) was attained in the ternary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) (Figure 4d). Moreover, the trap-assisted or Shockley–Read–Hall recombination in the ternary blend is further suppressed with increasing IBC-F content in the host blend, and the smallest slope of \(1.003\ k T/q\) was obtained for the ternary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:1, w/w) (as shown in Table S4 and Figure S8 in the Supporting Information), indicating that the trap-assisted recombination evolves to a free-carrier bimolecular. Indeed, bimolecular recombination is weak in all of the ternary devices with an \(\alpha\) greater than 0.92 (as shown in Table S4 and Figure S8 in the Supporting Information). We subsequently applied the transient photovoltage technique to determine the charge carrier lifetimes \((\tau)\) of the devices. As shown in Figure S9 in the Supporting Information, the ternary device
with \(20\%\) IBC-F exhibited a longer \(\tau\) of \(0.878\,\upmu\mathrm{s}\) than the binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) \((\tau\,=\,0.868\,\mathrm{~\upmu s})\) . Higher \(\tau\) of 1.052 and \(1.514~\upmu\mathrm{s}\) was observed in the ternary blend with \(50\%\) and \(100\%\) IBC-F, respectively, indicating the incorporation of IBC-F in the PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend has positive effect in enhancing its charge carrier lifetime, which is beneficial for obtaining higher FF values of the resulting devices. Thus, the synergetic effect of IBC-F on weakening the bimolecular and trap-assisted recombination and enhancing the carrier lifetimes of PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend results in the higher FF of the ternary devices.
The ordered face-on molecular packing orientation and favorable well-defined fibrillar D/A network are critical to obtain a high FF in PSCs. We performed 2D grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (2D-GIWAXS) measurements to investigate the influence of IBC-F on the molecular packing properties of the ternary blend. As shown in Figure S10 in Supporting Information, both neat PBDB-T and IBC-F films exhibit a wide (010) diffraction halo, suggesting mixed orientations in the solid film for both materials. In contrast, IE4F-S exhibits dominant face-on molecular orientations, which is beneficial for efficient charge transport in PSCs.[53,54] The pristine PBDB-T film showed a \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking (010) peak and lamellar packing diffraction (100) peak in the out-of-plane (OOP) direction at 1.61 and \(0.34\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) , with crystal coherence length (CCL) estimated to be 0.84 and \(0.50\;\mathrm{nm}\) , respectively. A stronger (010) signal at \(1.78\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) \((\mathrm{CCL}=1.13\ \mathrm{nm})\) and lamellar signal at \(0.47\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) \((\mathrm{CCL}=6.90\ \mathrm{nm})\) were observed in the OOP direction of the pristine IE4F-S film. For the pristine IBC-F film, the face-on \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking is indicated by the scattering of the (010) signal at \(1.50\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) in the OOP direction, with CCL of \(0.86\ \mathrm{nm}\) . The PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) binary blend showed a lamellar stacking (100) peak both in the in-plane direction at \(0.29\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) (IP, \(\mathrm{CCL}\,=\,6.53\,\mathrm{\nm})\) and OOP direction at \(0.33\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) \((\mathrm{CCL}=6.02\ \mathrm{nm})\) , along with preferable (010) peaks at \(1.77\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) in OOP \((\mathrm{CCL}=0.90\ \mathrm{nm})\) (as shown in Figure 5). Interestingly, the ternary blends show multiple diffraction peaks, indicating improved ordering of these materials. As shown in Figure 5, the CCL values of the lamellar stacking (100) peaks increased from 6.02 to \(10.53\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the ternary blend when incorporating \(20\%\) IBC-F in the binary host blend. Meanwhile, a higher CCL of \(0.97\;\mathrm{nm}\) was also observed in the face-on \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking (010) peak in OOP direction for
the PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) compared to the control binary blend \((\mathrm{CCL}=0.90)\). Therefore, the incorporation of \(20\%\) IBC-F could effectively enhance the molecular interactions to facilitate preferential face-on orientation in the blend film, resulting in improved charge transport in the ternary PSC.
Atomic force microscopy (AFM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurements were carried out to study the impact of IBC-F on the morphology of the ternary active layers. As shown in Figure 6a,b, the PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) and PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w) blends exhibited similar and rather smooth surfaces with a root-mean-square roughness \(\left(R_{\mathrm{q}}\right)\) of 2.50 and \(2.41\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively, implying good miscibility of \(20\%\) IBC-F with the PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend. However, further increasing the IBC-F ratios in the blend gradually leads to larger \(R_{\mathrm{q}}\) values (as shown in Figure S11 in the Supporting Information). Figure 6c,d shows TEM images of the blend films of PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) and PBDB-T:IE4F-S:IBC-F (1:1:0.2, w/w). Due to the different electron densities between donor and acceptor materials, the bright or dark region in the TEM image represents the donor-rich or acceptor-rich domains, respectively. Note that the PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) blend shows a well-developed fibrillar structure (Figure 6c). The ternary blend with \(20\%\) IBC-F shows more uniform bright and dark domains along with more evident fibrillar textures than the binary blend, implying the positive impact of \(20\%\) IBC-F on facilitating better morphology for obtaining a higher FF value. Clear phase separation with more obvious fibrillar textures but larger domain sizes were observed in the ternary films with
higher IBC-F contents (Figure S11 in the Supporting Information), which should correspond to the high FF values in the ternary PSCs with high IBC-F contents.
The morphology stability of the BHJ layer is a critical factor that significantly impacts the thermal and photoinduced stability of PSCs. Encouraged by the enhanced \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking coherence length and intermolecular interaction in the ternary blend with \(20\%\) IBC-F, we further evaluated the influence of IBC-F in the device stability. As shown in Figure 7a, a steep "burn-in" degradation, which only retains \(70\%\) of its initial PCE, was observed in the unencapsulated binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) under continuous thermal aging at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8.5\,\mathrm{h}\) in a nitrogen-filled glovebox. Then, the device performance recovers to \(82\%\) of its initial value after \(49\,\mathrm{h}\) thermal aging. The "burn-in" degradation is significantly restricted in the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F, in which PCE can remain at \(91.9\%\) under \(8.5\,\mathrm{h}\) thermal aging, and then recover to \(95.4\%\) after \(25\,\mathrm{h}\) thermal aging. Impressively, the ternary device retains a higher initial PCE of \(81\%\) compared to the host binary device \((71.7\%\) of its initial PCE) after \(1000\,\mathrm{h}\) continuous thermal aging at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is ascribed to the lower FF loss by incorporating \(20\%\) IBC-F (Figure S12 in the Supporting Information). In addition to improving the thermal stability, the incorporation of \(20\%\) IBC-F in the host blend is beneficial to promoting the photoinduced stability. As depicted in Figure 7b, the PCE of the binary device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S (1:1, w/w) decays to \(73.6\%\) of its initial, whereas the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F remained at \(85.5\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1000\,\mathrm{h}\) continuous illumination, resulting from the lower
FF loss (Figure S12, Supporting Information). These results indicate the positive effect of IBC-F on improving the thermal and photoinduced stability of the ternary device.
In conclusion, we designed and synthesized a new \(\mathrm{A}{-}\pi-\) \(\mathrm{D}{-}\pi{-}\mathrm{A}\) structured organic semiconductor molecule IBC-F containing the same IDT central fused ring D-unit with the acceptor material IE4F-S and bifluorobenzene \(\pi\) -bridge which enhances its ability to tune the morphology of the ternary active layer of the ternary PSCs. Even though the PBDB-T:IBC-F-based device exhibited extremely poor performance, IBC-F plays important role in improving the charge dissociation and extraction, suppressing bimolecular and trap-assisted recombination, increasing charge carriers lifetime, and enhancing intermolecular interactions with preferential face-on orientation, for the improved PCE of the ternary PSCs. Thus, a PCE as high as \(15.06\%\) was achieved in the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F, which is \(10\%\) greater than that of the control device based on PBDB-T:IE4F-S \((\mathrm{PCE}=13.70\%)\) ). More importantly, the ternary device with \(20\%\) IBC-F shows superior thermal and photoinduced stability over the control binary device. These results suggest that despite the extremely poor photovoltaic performance of IBC-F, the addition of IBC-F third component into the PBDB-T:IE4F-S blend not only improves the photovoltaic performance but also has a synergistic benefit on both the thermal aging and photoinduced stability, which demonstrates a significant advantage for developing highly efficient and stable PSCs for practical applications.
